---
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/tachyon-cms/tachyoncms';
---

<Base
  title="FAQ · TachyonCMS"
  description="Straight answers on TachyonCMS: is it production ready, what's the license, is there a CLA, how to report a security issue, and where the admin stands."
  path="/faq"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="FAQ"
    title="Questions, answered straight"
    lede="The things people ask first. Something missing? Ask in Discussions, or write to hello@tachyoncms.com."
  />

  <div class="faq">
    <Prose split>
      <h2 id="production">Is it production ready?</h2>
      <p>
        No. TachyonCMS is pre-alpha. The content API, sign-in and access rules work today. The admin screen, rich text,
        drafts and media don't exist yet, so keep it off public networks for now, and expect the occasional breaking change
        between 0.x releases.
      </p>

      <h2 id="license">What's the license?</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT</a>. Use it, change it and ship it, commercially or not. It stays open
        source, and <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/GOVERNANCE.md`}>GOVERNANCE.md</a> says so in writing.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cla">Do I have to sign a CLA to contribute?</h2>
      <p>
        No, and you never will. Nobody asks you to assign copyright either: you keep it. What you do instead is sign off
        each commit under the <a href="https://developercertificate.org/">Developer Certificate of Origin</a>, which is one
        flag: <code>git commit -s</code>. Because copyright stays spread across everyone who contributed, no single person or
        company can take the project closed.
      </p>

      <h2 id="security">How do I report a security issue?</h2>
      <p>
        Privately, please, never in a public issue. Use{' '}
        <a href={`${repo}/security/advisories/new`}>GitHub's private vulnerability reporting</a>, or email{' '}
        <a href="mailto:security@tachyoncms.com">security@tachyoncms.com</a> if you can't use GitHub. You'll get an
        acknowledgement within 3 working days. Fixes land on <code>main</code> and ship in the next 0.x release; older 0.x
        releases don't get backports. <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>SECURITY.md</a> has the details, and{' '}
        <a href="/security">the security page</a> covers how releases are signed.
      </p>

      <h2 id="admin">Where's the admin?</h2>
      <p>
        It isn't built yet. Today you work with TachyonCMS through its HTTP API and the <code>tachyon</code> command line.
        The admin comes after rich text and blocks, drafts and scheduling, media, live preview and authoring tools, and the
        bar for it is that anyone who has used WordPress can pick it up without training.{' '}
        <a href="/roadmap">The roadmap</a> has the order, and <a href="/editors">the editors page</a> will email you when it's
        ready.
      </p>

      <h2 id="runs-on">What does it run on?</h2>
      <p>
        One binary and one Postgres database. <a href="/releases">Releases</a> come as binaries for Linux (x86_64, arm64,
        static) and macOS (Intel, Apple silicon), and as a Docker image at{' '}
        <code>ghcr.io/tachyon-cms/tachyoncms</code>. Postgres is the only database it supports.
      </p>

      <h2 id="graphql">Is there GraphQL?</h2>
      <p>
        Not yet. The API is REST and JSON. GraphQL is one of the options in{' '}
        <a href={`${repo}/discussions/39`}>the poll on what comes next</a>, so if you need it, vote.
      </p>

      <h2 id="fast">How fast is it?</h2>
      <p>
        There are no numbers on this site, on purpose. It's Rust, and published content is read from a single row, which is
        why it should be quick. That's an argument from how it's built, not a number. I'll publish reproducible
        benchmarks against the popular CMSs, method and all, before I quote one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="hosted">Is there a hosted version?</h2>
      <p>No. You run it yourself, wherever a binary or a container can run next to Postgres.</p>

      <h2 id="who">Who's behind it?</h2>
      <p>
        Me, James Conway (<a href="https://github.com/jamesconwaydev">@jamesconwaydev</a>). I started TachyonCMS, and while
        the project is small I make the final call, in public. The <a href="/community">community page</a> has how that
        opens up as more people join.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">How do I get in touch?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Questions and ideas: <a href={`${repo}/discussions`}>GitHub Discussions</a></li>
        <li>Anything else: <a href="mailto:hello@tachyoncms.com">hello@tachyoncms.com</a></li>
        <li>Security: <a href="mailto:security@tachyoncms.com">security@tachyoncms.com</a></li>
        <li>Code of Conduct reports: <a href="mailto:conduct@tachyoncms.com">conduct@tachyoncms.com</a></li>
        <li>The name and logo: <a href="mailto:legal@tachyoncms.com">legal@tachyoncms.com</a></li>
        <li>Sponsoring: <a href="mailto:sponsors@tachyoncms.com">sponsors@tachyoncms.com</a></li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .faq {
    padding: 64px 0 clamp(3.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }
  .faq :global(.prose > h2:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
